1
00:00:03,390 --> 00:00:05,030
It's, like, the best one they make,

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:06,480
I just can't get it to work.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:06,900
I'll figure it out.

4
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:10,350
It streams HD video straight to your phone while it's flying.

5
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:11,480
Nice.

6
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:14,890
Where were you when I was single?

7
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:19,250
Okay, we should leave in about an hour. You all packed?

8
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:21,770
Uh, yeah, I just need to throw in a few last minute things--

9
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:24,240
you know, makeup, underwear, clothes.

10
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:27,450
If your bathroom floor counts as a carry-on,

11
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:29,490
you're packed.

12
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,560
Leonard, have you ever given a high school commencement speech before?

13
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:34,620
Nope. It's pretty exciting.

14
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:37,210
Aren't you afraid of being blinded?

15
00:00:37,210 --> 00:00:38,760
How would I be blinded?

16
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,120
At the end of the ceremony,

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,460
all the students throw those pointy hats in the air.

18
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:47,500
It's all pomp and circumstance until someone loses an eye.

19
00:00:48,390 --> 00:00:50,280
I'll take my chances.

20
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:51,120
Fine.

21
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:55,000
I wonder if they make "I told you so" cards in braille.

22
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:58,120
Look, the problem with commencement speeches

23
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:00,010
is that they're boring.

24
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,620
Ooh, do you own a T-shirt cannon?

25
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:05,020
Why would he own a T-shirt cannon?

26
00:01:05,020 --> 00:01:07,060
I don't know. Why do I own one?

27
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,110
I think it's really nice

28
00:01:09,110 --> 00:01:11,110
that you're sharing this experience with Penny.

29
00:01:11,110 --> 00:01:12,500
Mm, I thought it'd be fun

30
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:14,610
to show her my old stomping grounds.

31
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:17,860
I even know the exact spot where they used to stomp me.

32
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,590
Well, now you get to go back as a successful scientist.

33
00:01:21,590 --> 00:01:23,130
With a beautiful girl on your arm.

34
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:26,090
And a pointy hat sticking out of your eyeball.

35
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,330
How'd you get ready so fast?

36
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:56,360
Oh, I pack light.

37
00:01:56,360 --> 00:01:58,160
Once, I got through an entire spring break

38
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:01,180
with nothing but a long T-shirt and a belt.

39
00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:03,220
Why did you need a belt?

40
00:02:03,220 --> 00:02:05,870
It's called an evening look.

41
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:08,460
I've never been to New Jersey before.

42
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:10,560
It gets a bad rap from shows

43
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:12,800
like Jersey Shore and Real Housewives.

44
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,890
So it's not really like that?

45
00:02:13,890 --> 00:02:15,840
No, it's like that.

46
00:02:17,540 --> 00:02:19,240
Well, I'm excited to show you around.

47
00:02:19,240 --> 00:02:21,170
You think we'll have time to visit your mom over there?

48
00:02:21,170 --> 00:02:21,910
Yes.

49
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:24,270
We'll also have time to put my junk in a garlic press,

50
00:02:24,270 --> 00:02:26,380
but I'm not doing that either.

51
00:02:31,190 --> 00:02:32,350
I just got an alert.

52
00:02:32,350 --> 00:02:33,950
Our flight's been canceled.

53
00:02:33,950 --> 00:02:34,940
What?

54
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:38,040
Yeah, looks like there's a big storm all up the East Coast.

55
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:40,280
Well, can we get on another airline?

56
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,680
I don't think so.

57
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,180
So, that's it? We're not going?

58
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:45,530
I guess not.

59
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:47,650
- Well, that sucks. - Yeah.

60
00:02:47,650 --> 00:02:49,770
I worked hard on that speech, too.

61
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,470
Oh. You could tell it to me.

62
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:53,150
Oh, thank you, but I'm okay.

63
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:54,120
Are you sure?

64
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:55,730
I could pretend I'm a high school cheerleader

65
00:02:55,730 --> 00:02:59,180
who can't control herself around esteemed alumni.

66
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:02,760
Greetings, distinguished cheerleaders.

67
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:08,600
Okay, the WiFi extender is on, the camera's on,

68
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:09,800
they're both on the same network;

69
00:03:09,800 --> 00:03:11,020
we should be getting an image.

70
00:03:11,020 --> 00:03:13,540
All I see is a black screen.

71
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:15,940
And my own reflection.

72
00:03:16,380 --> 00:03:18,660
I look sad.

73
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:21,600
Maybe we should recalibrate it.

74
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:22,530
All right.

75
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:25,210
Step one: rapidly flip the calibration switch

76
00:03:25,210 --> 00:03:26,640
from the fully up to the fully down

77
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:29,520
positions for at least ten times.

78
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:32,250
It actually says "at least"?

79
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:33,330
Yeah.

80
00:03:33,330 --> 00:03:34,850
Why would they say "at least"?

81
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:36,560
Is it ten toggles?

82
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,550
Is it 100 toggles? You know?

83
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:40,050
Is it 1,000 toggles?

84
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:41,520
10,000 toggles?

85
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:43,900
- 100,000 toggles? - Sheldon!

86
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:48,210
You see where I'm going with this.

87
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:49,800
Just flip the switch

88
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:53,160
until the lights on the drone change to solid yellow.

89
00:03:53,160 --> 00:03:54,990
All right, that seems simple enough.

90
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:57,950
Initiating calibration sequence.

91
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:00,030
One, two, three, four,

92
00:04:00,030 --> 00:04:03,210
five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten.

93
00:04:06,410 --> 00:04:10,340
Well, I suppose ten is technically "at least ten."

94
00:04:12,010 --> 00:04:15,170
But they're still getting at least one angry letter.

95
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:19,300
Now I rotate it horizontally

96
00:04:19,300 --> 00:04:23,640
on its center axis until the lights turn green.

97
00:04:23,640 --> 00:04:26,900
Initiating rotation sequence.

98
00:04:27,410 --> 00:04:29,780
Don't look at me, initiate.

99
00:04:33,930 --> 00:04:35,190
What does red and yellow mean?

100
00:04:35,190 --> 00:04:36,950
It means the calibration failed.

101
00:04:36,950 --> 00:04:38,260
We have to start over.

102
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:39,670
Oh. Very well.

103
00:04:39,670 --> 00:04:42,250
Reinitiating calibration sequence.

104
00:04:42,250 --> 00:04:43,880
One, two, three, four,

105
00:04:43,880 --> 00:04:47,430
five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten...

106
00:04:49,600 --> 00:04:51,810
...eleven.

107
00:04:55,010 --> 00:04:57,630
It's a good thing I didn't send that letter.

108
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:05,840
Hello, Daddy. What's up?

109
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:08,520
Not much, just wanted to see how my son's doing.

110
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:10,400
Very well, thank you.

111
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,810
Are you still dating that dermatologist?

112
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:14,760
If you could feel how soft my skin is,

113
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:16,880
you wouldn't have to ask.

114
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:20,490
Oh, and there's something else I wanted to ask you.

115
00:05:20,490 --> 00:05:24,440
Why did you spend a month's rent on a toy helicopter?!

116
00:05:26,260 --> 00:05:29,060
Oh, you're where that bill goes.

117
00:05:29,580 --> 00:05:31,790
I'm tired of indulging your foolish lifestyle.

118
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:33,470
It's time you learned responsibility.

119
00:05:33,470 --> 00:05:34,860
And the only way to teach you that

120
00:05:34,860 --> 00:05:36,440
is to cut off your allowance.

121
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:38,260
No, Daddy, no!

122
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:40,850
There are lots of other ways to teach me responsibility.

123
00:05:40,850 --> 00:05:42,100
I know, you can give me an egg

124
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:44,770
and make me take care of it for a week!

125
00:05:45,360 --> 00:05:47,190
No, my mind is made up.

126
00:05:47,190 --> 00:05:48,630
Starting now, you're on your own.

127
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:52,540
But, Daddy, I-I miss my family so much,

128
00:05:52,540 --> 00:05:54,760
and with you and Mummy getting a divorce,

129
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,920
I feel sad and empty.

130
00:05:56,920 --> 00:05:59,200
Buying a little toy every now and then

131
00:05:59,200 --> 00:06:01,410
helps me fill that void.

132
00:06:01,410 --> 00:06:03,150
So while I can't hug you every day,

133
00:06:03,150 --> 00:06:04,470
flying that helicopter...

134
00:06:04,470 --> 00:06:06,780
I'm cutting you off.

135
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:10,510
Just to be clear, financially or mid-sentence?

136
00:06:12,970 --> 00:06:14,160
No red and yellow,

137
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:16,000
no red and yellow.

138
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:18,810
- It's green! - Yay! We did it!

139
00:06:20,230 --> 00:06:22,070
God, if it's this much fun to rotate,

140
00:06:22,070 --> 00:06:24,080
imagine when we fly it.

141
00:06:24,930 --> 00:06:27,470
Okay, now all I have to do is rotate it vertically

142
00:06:27,470 --> 00:06:30,490
until the lights turn off.

143
00:06:30,870 --> 00:06:32,470
Oh, no!

144
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:36,000
All right, playtime's over.

145
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,730
Let's open this baby up.

146
00:06:38,240 --> 00:06:39,970
Won't that void the warranty?

147
00:06:39,970 --> 00:06:44,090
Sheldon, I have a master's degree in engineering.

148
00:06:44,090 --> 00:06:47,430
I wipe my bottom with warranties.

149
00:06:49,260 --> 00:06:50,420
Except for AppleCare.

150
00:06:50,420 --> 00:06:53,420
That pays for itself in the long run.

151
00:06:56,500 --> 00:06:57,800
Hey, where'd you go?

152
00:06:57,800 --> 00:07:00,550
I got you a little something to cheer you up.

153
00:07:00,550 --> 00:07:01,480
Really?

154
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:03,330
Sex last night, pancakes this morning--

155
00:07:03,330 --> 00:07:04,880
Am I dying?

156
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:07,630
Just open it.

157
00:07:09,510 --> 00:07:10,390
A cap and gown?

158
00:07:10,390 --> 00:07:11,930
Why do I need a cap and gown?

159
00:07:11,930 --> 00:07:14,750
Because you are giving your commencement speech.

160
00:07:14,750 --> 00:07:15,880
What are you talking about?

161
00:07:15,880 --> 00:07:17,010
I called your old high school

162
00:07:17,010 --> 00:07:20,070
and convinced them to let you give your speech over Skype.

163
00:07:20,570 --> 00:07:21,680
Really?

164
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:22,650
That's amazing!

165
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:24,780
And you gave me the robes to give it in.

166
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:25,440
Thank you.

167
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:26,700
Yeah, now about those--

168
00:07:26,700 --> 00:07:28,120
uh, they came from a costume shop,

169
00:07:28,120 --> 00:07:30,360
and all they had left was sexy graduate,

170
00:07:30,360 --> 00:07:31,960
so they...

171
00:07:32,570 --> 00:07:34,780
might be a little short.

172
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:38,060
- Short and sexy, that's my wheelhouse. - Yeah!

173
00:07:47,900 --> 00:07:50,670
Boy, oh, boy, that's a lot of pieces.

174
00:07:51,660 --> 00:07:53,130
You know what they all do, right?

175
00:07:53,130 --> 00:07:55,180
Yes, of course.

176
00:07:55,730 --> 00:07:57,010
What about this one?

177
00:07:57,010 --> 00:07:59,530
Well, I... how familiar are you

178
00:07:59,530 --> 00:08:02,230
with miniaturized integrated logic circuits?

179
00:08:02,230 --> 00:08:02,990
Not very.

180
00:08:02,990 --> 00:08:06,040
That right there is a miniaturized integrated logic circuit.

181
00:08:09,170 --> 00:08:10,750
So, can you get it working?

182
00:08:10,750 --> 00:08:13,160
I'm an MIT-trained engineer.

183
00:08:13,160 --> 00:08:16,220
I've built components for the space station.

184
00:08:16,220 --> 00:08:19,500
I thought the zero-gravity toilet didn't work.

185
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:21,280
It worked fine,

186
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:24,100
it just wasn't designed for Russian cosmonauts

187
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,310
and their potato-based diet.

188
00:08:27,310 --> 00:08:28,560
Hey, guys.

189
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:29,600
Hello.

190
00:08:29,600 --> 00:08:31,770
I have to return the helicopter. My father...

191
00:08:31,770 --> 00:08:34,600
What did you do?!

192
00:08:36,310 --> 00:08:37,300
Well, don't worry.

193
00:08:37,300 --> 00:08:38,900
He went to MIT.

194
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:40,650
He can solve any problem,

195
00:08:40,650 --> 00:08:44,730
as long as it doesn't originate in a Russian man's colon.

196
00:08:50,390 --> 00:08:51,700
I don't freaking believe this!

197
00:08:51,700 --> 00:08:53,280
Relax, it'll be fine.

198
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:55,500
No, you have to put this back together right now,

199
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:56,750
so I can return it!

200
00:08:56,750 --> 00:08:58,390
You can't return it.

201
00:08:58,390 --> 00:09:01,420
Howard wiped his bottom with the warranty.

202
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:03,080
What?!

203
00:09:03,080 --> 00:09:05,790
I think metaphorically.

204
00:09:05,790 --> 00:09:08,860
But he was in the bathroom for a while.

205
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:11,440
Howard, my father cut me off.

206
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:12,850
I have to get my money back for this!

207
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:14,060
Calm down.

208
00:09:14,060 --> 00:09:14,980
Okay.

209
00:09:15,380 --> 00:09:17,040
Okay, you're right.

210
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:18,930
It's time for me to step up

211
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:21,310
and take responsibility for my life.

212
00:09:21,680 --> 00:09:23,400
Be a man.

213
00:09:25,330 --> 00:09:27,570
Hello, Mummy.

214
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:29,800
Hello, Rajesh.

215
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:31,450
What a nice surprise.

216
00:09:31,450 --> 00:09:33,230
Well, I've been thinking about you.

217
00:09:33,230 --> 00:09:36,060
How are you doing? Are you happy, Mummy?

218
00:09:36,760 --> 00:09:38,830
Such a sweet boy for asking.

219
00:09:38,830 --> 00:09:42,700
Can't believe you come from the poison seed of your father.

220
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:45,380
Well, I like to think I take mostly after you.

221
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:46,570
Anyway, speaking of Daddy,

222
00:09:46,570 --> 00:09:48,990
I had a very strange conversation with him.

223
00:09:48,990 --> 00:09:51,100
He said he couldn't afford to send me money anymore

224
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:54,050
because of his active social life.

225
00:09:54,050 --> 00:09:57,240
What does that mean, "active social life"?

226
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:59,150
Well, let's not talk about him

227
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:01,480
or whatever shenanigans he may or may not be up to.

228
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:03,010
Let's talk about you.

229
00:10:03,890 --> 00:10:06,370
Rajesh, is your father seeing someone?

230
00:10:06,370 --> 00:10:09,470
All I know, Mummy, is that he's a single wealthy doctor,

231
00:10:09,470 --> 00:10:10,900
and now, for some reason,

232
00:10:10,900 --> 00:10:14,300
there's no money for your little boy.

233
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:18,620
Well, however much money your father was giving you,

234
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:19,650
I'll give you more.

235
00:10:19,650 --> 00:10:21,800
Thank you, Mummy, I love you!

236
00:10:21,800 --> 00:10:24,890
Helicopters for everybody!

237
00:10:26,430 --> 00:10:27,900
What's taking so long?

238
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:29,890
I don't think this is gonna work.

239
00:10:29,890 --> 00:10:31,880
Just let me see.

240
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:39,340
Sweetie, you know you're supposed to wear clothes

241
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:41,240
underneath a graduation gown.

242
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:44,040
A: surprised you know that.

243
00:10:44,370 --> 00:10:48,100
B: I wanted to look like a sexy graduate for you.

244
00:10:48,520 --> 00:10:49,520
Well, you do.

245
00:10:49,520 --> 00:10:52,080
- Thank you. - Yeah.

246
00:10:52,950 --> 00:10:55,220
You're gonna be on Skype; they're not gonna see your legs.

247
00:10:55,220 --> 00:10:57,420
Well, I'm gonna go put on some pants just in case.

248
00:10:57,420 --> 00:11:01,340
But I have to say, this is very freeing.

249
00:11:02,280 --> 00:11:05,420
Add a belt and I'll take you someplace nice.

250
00:11:06,230 --> 00:11:09,010
Done and done.

251
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:17,030
Okay, I think I've narrowed it down

252
00:11:17,030 --> 00:11:20,790
to a faulty pin on the onboard communication chip.

253
00:11:20,790 --> 00:11:23,050
Very impressive.

254
00:11:23,050 --> 00:11:25,300
You know, when you're done with that,

255
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:28,140
can you look at this?

256
00:11:29,290 --> 00:11:31,890
It doesn't make smoke anymore.

257
00:11:31,890 --> 00:11:33,680
One toy at a time.

258
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:34,880
Mm. Sorry.

259
00:11:34,880 --> 00:11:36,640
Ah, maybe it's for the best.

260
00:11:36,640 --> 00:11:38,450
I hear locomotive smoke

261
00:11:38,450 --> 00:11:42,150
is the second leading cause of death among train aficionados.

262
00:11:42,730 --> 00:11:43,920
What's the first one?

263
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:45,750
Suicide.

264
00:11:46,650 --> 00:11:47,850
Wrong.

265
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:50,180
Obesity.

266
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,040
I have the tool you wanted.

267
00:11:53,040 --> 00:11:54,430
Thank you.

268
00:11:54,430 --> 00:11:56,270
You guys don't have to go to the trouble.

269
00:11:56,270 --> 00:11:57,250
I'm back in the money now;

270
00:11:57,250 --> 00:11:59,170
I can just buy another helicopter.

271
00:11:59,170 --> 00:12:00,220
It's not about the money.

272
00:12:00,220 --> 00:12:01,620
It's about solving a problem.

273
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:03,200
It's why I became an engineer.

274
00:12:03,200 --> 00:12:05,750
It's what I like to do, it's what I'm trained to do.

275
00:12:05,750 --> 00:12:06,880
It's who I am.

276
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:11,460
Oh, look at you-- the little engineer that could.

277
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:14,100
Why don't you just call tech support?

278
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:16,980
- Hey! - Not cool!

279
00:12:16,980 --> 00:12:17,740
What?

280
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:19,510
There's two kinds of people in this world:

281
00:12:19,510 --> 00:12:20,980
those who call tech support

282
00:12:20,980 --> 00:12:24,310
and those who make fun of the people who call tech support.

283
00:12:24,310 --> 00:12:26,150
I call tech support all the time.

284
00:12:26,150 --> 00:12:28,640
- You call tech support! - What a baby!

285
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:33,210
And now, for our commencement address,

286
00:12:33,210 --> 00:12:35,900
one of our most distinguished alumni,

287
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:41,050
noted Caltech physicist Dr. Leonard Hofstadter.

288
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:46,840
Members of the faculty, students,

289
00:12:46,840 --> 00:12:49,420
I'm excited to speak to you today.

290
00:12:49,420 --> 00:12:52,620
I can't help but remember the last time I was in this auditorium.

291
00:12:52,620 --> 00:12:54,310
Two guys from the lacrosse team

292
00:12:54,310 --> 00:12:57,340
played keep-away with my asthma inhaler.

293
00:12:57,340 --> 00:13:01,180
But enough about my ten-year reunion.

294
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:03,740
I'd also like to take a moment

295
00:13:03,740 --> 00:13:05,820
to thank my beautiful fiancee

296
00:13:05,820 --> 00:13:07,280
for to helping make this speech possible,

297
00:13:07,280 --> 00:13:10,160
even though weather nearly prevented it.

298
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:11,910
Oh. Hello.

299
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:13,550
I-I didn't know he was gonna point it at me,

300
00:13:13,550 --> 00:13:14,750
so...

301
00:13:14,750 --> 00:13:16,580
don't do drugs and stay in school.

302
00:13:16,580 --> 00:13:17,580
They're graduating.

303
00:13:17,580 --> 00:13:19,670
Okay, bye!

304
00:13:21,870 --> 00:13:23,720
All right, the power supply is reconnected.

305
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:24,850
I think we're back in business.

306
00:13:24,850 --> 00:13:27,760
Let's just run a few tests before we take it outside.

307
00:13:27,760 --> 00:13:28,850
Sheldon, we got WiFi?

308
00:13:28,850 --> 00:13:29,510
Check.

309
00:13:29,510 --> 00:13:30,110
GPS?

310
00:13:30,110 --> 00:13:30,770
Check.

311
00:13:30,770 --> 00:13:31,690
Battery charged?

312
00:13:31,690 --> 00:13:32,360
Check.

313
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:36,070
Four hours of our lives gone? Check.

314
00:13:36,790 --> 00:13:38,230
All right. All systems go.

315
00:13:38,230 --> 00:13:39,560
In five...

316
00:13:39,560 --> 00:13:43,060
...four, three, two, one.

317
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:50,550
That's what my train used to do.

318
00:13:53,320 --> 00:13:55,330
Ready to call tech support?

319
00:13:55,870 --> 00:13:57,770
Give me the number.

320
00:13:58,890 --> 00:14:02,640
Probably wind up talking to some foreign guy

321
00:14:02,640 --> 00:14:05,770
who's reading from the same manual I have.

322
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:17,050
It's my father, you jerks.

323
00:14:20,040 --> 00:14:21,160
Hello, Daddy.

324
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:22,600
What did you say to your mother?

325
00:14:22,600 --> 00:14:23,330
Nothing.

326
00:14:23,330 --> 00:14:25,110
I was just calling to check in,

327
00:14:25,110 --> 00:14:26,560
make sure she's doing okay.

328
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:27,720
Well, after talking to you,

329
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:30,760
she seems to think I'm some sort of playboy.

330
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:32,200
Really?

331
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:35,240
I don't know where she'd get an idea like that.

332
00:14:35,650 --> 00:14:38,230
You know mummy and her crazy imagination.

333
00:14:38,230 --> 00:14:41,150
I'm so lucky I take after you.

334
00:14:41,950 --> 00:14:44,380
You think you take after me?

335
00:14:44,870 --> 00:14:46,270
Well, I try to.

336
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:47,980
I certainly wouldn't be a scientist

337
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:51,370
if you hadn't been my role model.

338
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:57,890
Who wants to go to Vegas in a real helicopter?!

339
00:14:59,570 --> 00:15:01,730
It was L. Frank Baum who said,

340
00:15:01,730 --> 00:15:06,590
"No thief, however skillful, can rob one of knowledge,

341
00:15:06,590 --> 00:15:11,700
and that is why knowledge is the best and safest treasure."

342
00:15:11,700 --> 00:15:15,650
Wow, I'm boring myself.

343
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:19,970
Sorry, I can't see any of your faces right now,

344
00:15:19,970 --> 00:15:22,470
but I bet they look like this.

345
00:15:25,510 --> 00:15:29,750
Uh, you know, I-I wrote an entire speech

346
00:15:29,750 --> 00:15:32,430
to say how high school prepares you

347
00:15:32,430 --> 00:15:34,940
and what a wonderful place it is,

348
00:15:34,940 --> 00:15:37,300
but I hated it.

349
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:41,260
Maybe high school's great if you look like this...

350
00:15:42,690 --> 00:15:47,400
...but I didn't even feel like I existed at that school.

351
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:49,570
And now that I think about it,

352
00:15:49,570 --> 00:15:51,800
I bet a lot of you feel the same way.

353
00:15:51,800 --> 00:15:56,340
So, for the remainder of my speech,

354
00:15:56,340 --> 00:15:59,030
this is for the invisible kids.

355
00:15:59,030 --> 00:16:02,100
Uh, maybe you never fit in.

356
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,530
Or maybe you're the smallest kid in the school,

357
00:16:04,530 --> 00:16:06,790
or the heaviest or the weirdest.

358
00:16:06,790 --> 00:16:07,890
Maybe you're graduating

359
00:16:07,890 --> 00:16:10,330
and you still haven't had your first kiss.

360
00:16:10,330 --> 00:16:12,780
By the way, 19;

361
00:16:12,780 --> 00:16:16,890
and Geraldine Coco, wherever you are, thank you.

362
00:16:18,010 --> 00:16:19,740
Maybe you don't have any friends.

363
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:22,640
And guess what? That's okay.

364
00:16:22,970 --> 00:16:25,250
While all the popular kids are off doing--

365
00:16:25,250 --> 00:16:26,810
whatever, I don't know what they were doing

366
00:16:26,810 --> 00:16:28,150
'cause I was never there...

367
00:16:28,150 --> 00:16:29,970
I'll-I'll tell you later.

368
00:16:31,230 --> 00:16:33,480
My point is,

369
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:35,440
while you're spending all this time on your own,

370
00:16:35,440 --> 00:16:39,210
building computers or practicing your cello,

371
00:16:39,210 --> 00:16:40,780
what you're really doing

372
00:16:40,780 --> 00:16:43,480
is becoming interesting.

373
00:16:43,480 --> 00:16:46,280
And when people finally do notice you,

374
00:16:46,280 --> 00:16:49,700
they're gonna find someone a lot cooler than they thought.

375
00:16:49,700 --> 00:16:52,250
And for those of you who were popular in high school,

376
00:16:52,250 --> 00:16:54,880
it's over, sorry.

377
00:16:55,510 --> 00:16:58,570
Thank you and congratulations.

378
00:17:02,370 --> 00:17:04,330
Your call is important to us.

379
00:17:04,330 --> 00:17:07,220
All our technicians are busy helping other customers.

380
00:17:07,220 --> 00:17:08,750
Please stay on the line

381
00:17:08,750 --> 00:17:11,340
and someone will be with you shortly.

382
00:17:21,070 --> 00:17:22,970
What happened to me?

383
00:17:25,380 --> 00:17:27,830
When did I become an old man

384
00:17:27,830 --> 00:17:31,170
baffled by modern technology?

385
00:17:31,170 --> 00:17:32,040
Next thing you know,

386
00:17:32,040 --> 00:17:34,930
I'll be hitching my pants up to my armpits

387
00:17:34,930 --> 00:17:36,470
and complaining about

388
00:17:36,470 --> 00:17:39,690
the awful music the kids are listening to.

389
00:17:39,690 --> 00:17:42,610
It is awful, isn't it?

390
00:17:42,610 --> 00:17:45,390
Listen to that noise.

391
00:17:48,710 --> 00:17:49,740
Hang on, hang on.

392
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:51,230
It's working! I did it!

393
00:17:51,230 --> 00:17:52,000
How'd you do that?

394
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,510
No idea, but I did it!

395
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,020
Maybe you shouldn't be flying it inside.

396
00:17:58,020 --> 00:17:59,230
Well, I'm not flying it!

397
00:17:59,230 --> 00:18:00,220
Then who is?

398
00:18:00,220 --> 00:18:01,070
I don't know!

399
00:18:01,070 --> 00:18:04,540
Must be getting a WiFi signal from somewhere else!

400
00:18:07,270 --> 00:18:10,680
Initiate landing sequence! Initiate landing sequence!

401
00:18:11,480 --> 00:18:12,900
Hey, the camera's working!

402
00:18:12,900 --> 00:18:14,260
Oh, look, it's me.

403
00:18:17,340 --> 00:18:18,660
Tech support, can I help you?

404
00:18:18,660 --> 00:18:22,870
Yes, it's a robot uprising! Call the police!

405
00:18:30,260 --> 00:18:31,460
So you really think they liked it?

406
00:18:31,460 --> 00:18:33,870
Oh, sweetie, it was the best speech I...

407
00:18:37,150 --> 00:18:39,610
Don't worry, everyone in here is safe.

